{"reference":"Jeremiah 32","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":32,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Judgment and a Future Return","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter holds devastation and hope together rather than treating one as a denial of the other. Jeremiah buys a field while Jerusalem is under siege and while he announces its fall; the purchase, its witnessed deeds, and their preservation point toward the later promise that fields will again be bought. Within Jeremiah 30–33, this scene belongs with promises of healing, return, rebuilding, replanting, forgiveness, covenant renewal, and Davidic hope. The promised future does not erase the loss and judgment described here.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 32:1-44"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_032_04a8dc15cc3b2c813104763a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Jeremiah 32 in the Book","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Jeremiah is a varied book, bringing together poetry, prose sermons, narratives, letters, headings, repeated accounts, restoration collections, oracles concerning nations, and an appendix through a long compositional and textual history. In a practical outline of the received Masoretic form, chapters 30–33 form a restoration collection. This placement helps frame the chapter’s movement from siege and judgment toward return and renewed life in the land.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_032_022ebe8c9c38b6d347ed574a"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}